{"source_file": "/content/drive/MyDrive/Agronomy_Data_Source/ca8167en.pdf", "source_name": "ca8167en.pdf", "page_start": 69, "page_end": 69, "section": null, "extraction_method": "pymupdf", "text": "Table 3\nSample of an FFS\ncurriculum with soil\nhealth exercises\nintegrated\n➔ Weeks 8-15\nand Post-FFS\n8 BUILDING A FARMER FIELD SCHOOL CURRICULUM\nThe table below shows an example of a curriculum where some of the exercises presented in this manual\nare integrated. The curriculum does not include them all but only a selection of those that the facilitator\nand participants considered instrumental to improve their soils (the rest can be found in Annex II). If\nfarmers are facing specific soil issues then dedicated sections should be included. In other words, by\nfollowing the problem tree analysis, the group will consider different exercises and topics to include in\ntheir curricula in order to experiment with innovative practices suited to their specific context. If during\nthe introductory activities the exercises reported in this manual don’t fit your needs, consult the reference\nmaterial presented in the previous chapter for more ideas. Once again what is shown below is only an example! Activities\nPre-season\nTransect walk, interview key informants and families\nVillage Resource Map\nFocus group discussion, Farming/Soil problem analysis\nCommunity feedbacking meeting, Outline curriculum and PTDs (Ex. 11)\nArrange for a 1000 m2 Study Field within easy reach of the FFS participants\nPrepare seed-bed and seedlings for 1000 m2 to be ready in time for the first FFS session\nWeek 1\nOpening ceremony with introductions\nBallot-box pre-test and expectations\nPlanting of Study Field by FFS participants and trainers\nWeek 2\nIce-breakers exercise (for example, Throw a ball)\nEcosystem\nSpecial topic – Exercise 1. Soil composition\nWeek 3\nAgro-Ecosystem Analysis– Soil texture (Ex. 2)\nMineral fraction and water (Ex. 3)\nRoots/Vessels & Pesticides\nWeek 4\nAgro-Ecosystem 